-- Accepted Bus verb registry, schema 4. Each component registers its verbs
-- here when it enters the tree (AGENTS.md §5). tools/verb_registry.mix keeps
-- it canonical: one entry per line, fields in the order name, owner,
-- version, status, class, schema, with no comments inside the verbs list. Status is
-- accepted, draft or deprecated; versions are release versions
-- (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH).
--
-- Schema 4 adds `schema` to every verb and family, last: a serde request
-- type deriving JsonSchema (crate::Type), none (no body or {}), or pending
-- through 2026-11-30 inclusive in UTC. The gate refuses pending after that date.
-- A row may name command::ExecuteRequest only when its verb body is exactly {id, params?}; term and the bridged family migrate in later steps.
--
-- Schema 2 added `class` (docs/docs/decisions/2026-10-11-verb-classes.md):
-- intent (changes what a service holds or another caller sees), query
-- (changes nothing another caller could observe; it may write the artefact
-- it returns, as a capture does), input (synthetic low-level input) or
-- system (session plumbing, such as <app>.show). A verb the app describes as
-- read_only is a query. When in doubt, it is intent.
--
-- The toolkit drive verbs (docs/docs/drive.md) are one family that every egui
-- app answers under its own prefix: <app>.ui.tree, .ui.click, .ui.pointer,
-- .ui.scroll, .ui.key, .ui.type, .ui.menu, .ui.menu.list, .ui.menu.open,
-- .ui.menu.close, .ui.capture, <app>.window and
-- <app>.window.state. Each native app registers its concrete names, owned
-- by the app that answers them. Schema 3 added the families list below.
-- HELP and app.describe are protocol verbs every service answers; they are
-- not registered per component.
{
